xnulinux's own corecrypto interface in place of the corecrypto XNU ships.

scripts/vm-xnu-rebuild.sh removes EXTERNAL_HEADERS/corecrypto, osfmk/corecrypto and osfmk/arm64/corecrypto (files under
Apple's corecrypto internal-use license) and copies in xnu-patches/corecrypto: the interface's headers, written for
xnulinux with the layouts a crypto kext built for this kernel or for macOS agrees on, and osfmk/xnulinux_crypto, the
kernel's side of it. The kernel keeps no cryptographic algorithm: digests and HMAC go to the registered provider (the
corecrypto kext), modes through their tables, and the early random numbers (early_random, read_erandom before the kernel
PRNG registers) come from the host's generator; the four boot seeds are the booter's 256 bytes as they are (xnu.ko fills
them from Linux's get_random_bytes). Early random numbers need the host: this kernel no longer boots on hardware.


